WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.290
Film Courage: Would you say you still spend
300 days a year writing EMPIRE?

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.210
Danny Strong: No.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.250
I was the first two seasons of EMPIRE.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:13.850
I was very active and then with season three
I switched to consulting and made REBEL IN

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:14.850
THE RYE.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:20.210
So now with seasons three and four, I’m
still the executive producer on it, but I’m

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:22.840
not nearly as hands on as I was for the first
two seasons.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:27.710
Film Courage: You probably couldn’t have
written EMPIRE and REBEL IN THE RYE at the

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:28.710
same time?

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:33.180
Danny Strong: Well I think I wrote REBEL IN
THE RYE the first couple drafts before EMPIRE

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.320
happened.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.390
And actually EMPIRE happening slowed my progress
in getting REBEL IN THE RYE made because EMPIRE

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:40.649
got made.

00:00:40.649 --> 00:00:41.739
So I ended up working on that.

00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:46.170
But I ended up directing a lot of episodes
of EMPIRE, so that was an amazing experience

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.810
for me to transition into directing.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:52.840
It just couldn’t have been a better way
to really learn that craft because you have

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:58.890
to move so quickly and I have plenty more
to learn but it was a great start for me.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:02.220
Film Courage: When you are writing…and I
understand you have this writing day where

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:09.569
you have your breakfast, get a lot of “housekeeping”
out of the way, but is it easier to write

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:10.799
in New York versus LA?

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:12.100
Or the place doesn’t really matter?

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.149
Danny Strong: Well I love New York City.

00:01:14.149 --> 00:01:15.270
I love being there.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:20.689
I’m just so happy there, so for me I can
write anywhere and that’s kind of one of

00:01:20.689 --> 00:01:24.859
the fun things about writing is I can be anywhere
in the world and I can write.

00:01:24.859 --> 00:01:30.189
And it’s actually pretty cool to be in some
random city some where in some random coffee

00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:34.610
shop and you’re writing and it’s like
how often does someone have a job that they

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.140
can do anywhere in the world?

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.529
I mean that’s a lot of fun!

00:01:38.529 --> 00:01:41.869
And I’m perfectly productive in Los Angeles.

00:01:41.869 --> 00:01:46.329
I just love being in New York and it’s just
an exciting place to be.

00:01:46.329 --> 00:01:51.860
Film Courage: Can you relate to J.D. Salinger’s
process in terms of where once he crossed

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:55.779
over into meditation and all of that and he
needed to really barricade himself?

00:01:55.779 --> 00:01:57.209
Can you relate to this?

00:01:57.209 --> 00:02:00.289
Danny Strong: I definitely don’t barricade
myself at all.

00:02:00.289 --> 00:02:01.950
I actually write in public places.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:05.770
I don’t like being in a quiet room by myself.

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:13.280
I have a certain amount of ADD and being in
a quiet room I zone out and I’m less productive.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.700
So I actually like having people around me.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.739
I have my headset on, I listen to music.

00:02:17.739 --> 00:02:19.870
I write to movie scores.

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:24.349
So I’m just listening to movie scores and
people are around and there’s an energy

00:02:24.349 --> 00:02:26.800
and I find it very helpful.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.270
And it just helps me focus, but that’s just
me.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:40.370
So the isolation of what Mr. Salinger did
is kind of the opposite of how I work.

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:45.909
Film Courage: For new writers, I know they
are told to get feedback from other people.

00:02:45.909 --> 00:02:49.909
I was recently reading Anne Lamott’s Bird
By Bird and she talked about writing conferences

00:02:49.909 --> 00:02:57.150
and how they can be helpful, but at the same
time the slashing to the edits is also stabbing

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.469
the screenwriter a little bit.

00:02:58.469 --> 00:03:02.530
Can you talk about the kindest way to give
someone honest feedback?

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:05.930
So you are actually helping them, but you
are not wounding them so they won’t go on

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:06.930
[to write].

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:12.420
Danny Strong: Well I think that the best way
to give feedback and I very much rely on feedback

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:16.879
and I need notes from people and it helps
me see problems and it’s a very important

00:03:16.879 --> 00:03:19.900
part of my process is getting feedback.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:25.819
But I think to keep…I mean good feedback
is when it’s just about the script.

00:03:25.819 --> 00:03:27.239
It doesn’t get personal.

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:32.780
It doesn’t talk about bigger ideas of whether
you can do this or not or you have talent

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:33.780
or not.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:39.560
Where it’s literally about how to make this
script or this story better in areas that

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:41.120
are problematic.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:47.159
I think it’s really important when someone
is getting feedback that they talk to a few

00:03:47.159 --> 00:03:48.219
people.

00:03:48.219 --> 00:03:51.890
That they get feedback from maybe three people.

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:56.139
Because there could be feedback that someone
gives you that they are very passionate about

00:03:56.139 --> 00:03:57.840
but they don’t know what they’re talking
about.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.340
It’s bad feedback and they’re just not
good at giving feedback.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:04.319
There’s a lot of people that aren’t good
at giving feedback, right?

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:07.430
So you want a few opinions.

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:13.150
And for me when I start hearing from two or
three people the same problem they’re having,

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:17.970
well there’s probably a problem there and
there is probably something I need to address.

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:23.349
And one of my favorite parts of writing is
when I take a problem and then I fix it and

00:04:23.349 --> 00:04:24.819
then it’s good.

00:04:24.819 --> 00:04:30.060
That’s so exciting because you have something
that was bad that’s now good and it’s

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:34.639
like you have a hole and instead of a ditch,
you have a statue there instead.

00:04:34.639 --> 00:04:39.919
So I can only get there by getting feedback
from people.

